<p class="note rv">The goal was never more traffic for its own sake. It was to be the first result a German shopper sees, in Google or in an AI answer, at the moment they decide to buy.</p>

<section class="story rv">
  <h2><span class="mono">01</span>The challenge</h2>
  <div class="txt">
    <p>The store sells paint-by-numbers kits, including personalised kits made from the customer’s own photo. Demand in Germany is huge, but the search results were crowded with marketplaces, big craft retailers and aggregator blogs.</p>
    <p>The site had good products and almost no organic presence for the terms that sell them. Category and product pages competed with each other for the same keywords. The blog answered questions nobody was asking. And with few quality backlinks, the domain didn’t have the authority to move into the top three.</p>
    <p>At the same time, more and more shoppers were asking ChatGPT, Perplexity and Google’s AI Overviews for gift ideas and “how does it work” answers. The brand appeared in none of them.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story rv">
  <h2><span class="mono">02</span>The approach</h2>
  <div class="txt">
    <p>Three workstreams ran in parallel, all mapped to buying intent rather than raw search volume.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><div class="k">1</div><h3>Content strategy revamp</h3><p>Rebuilt the keyword map around intent: one clear page per commercial query, consolidated cannibalising pages, and rewrote category and product copy around how Germans actually search (“eigenes Bild”, “für Erwachsene”, “Foto”).</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="k">2</div><h3>Helpful content hub</h3><p>Replaced thin blog posts with guides that answer real pre-purchase questions, like colour-mixing charts, difficulty levels and gift guides, and linked them into the products they support.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="k">3</div><h3>Link building &amp; PR</h3><p>An outreach and digital PR campaign earned relevant links and mentions from German-language craft, lifestyle and gifting publications, building the authority needed to compete for the head terms.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="k">4</div><h3>SEO / AEO optimization</h3><p>Product and FAQ structured data, entity-rich copy, clean internal linking and answer-first formatting, so pages were easy for both Google and AI assistants to understand, quote and recommend.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story rv">
  <h2><span class="mono">03</span>The results</h2>
  <div class="txt">
    <p>Growth started within the first months and kept compounding. By November 2026 the store ranked in the top 20 for roughly 800 keywords, up from about 150 in May. Over 300 of those were in the top three.</p>
    <figure class="shot">
      <img src="/assets/cases/de-ecommerce/keyword-growth-card.webp" alt="Chart of organic keywords by ranking position, May to November 2026, with #1 and #2 positions for key terms" width="1100" height="746" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Organic keywords by position (top 3, 4–10, 11–20), May–Nov 2026, plus positions on 4 Nov 2026.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Estimated traffic grew across every intent type. The biggest gains came where they matter most for revenue: transactional and commercial searches, each above 22,000 estimated visits a month by November.</p>
    <figure class="shot">
      <img src="/assets/cases/de-ecommerce/intent-growth-card.webp" alt="Chart of estimated organic traffic by search intent from December 2025 to November 2026" width="1100" height="739" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Estimated organic traffic by search intent, Dec 2025 – Nov 2026.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story rv">
  <h2><span class="mono">04</span>Keyword wins</h2>
  <div class="txt">
    <p>Year over year, the store moved into the top positions for the most valuable paint-by-numbers searches in Germany:</p>
